--- a/docs/architecture/versioning.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/versioning.md
@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@ How `callisto version` turns changesets into file edits, and where interrupted-c
 A crash or a failed lockfile refresh can stop apply after some files are written. Applying the same plan again is file-level idempotent, and a fresh run recovers that plan from a checkpoint ([ADR 13](../adr/0013-interrupted-version-runs-resume-from-a-checkpoint-ref.md)):

 - Before its first write, `version` records `HEAD`, the index and the workspace's working tree (untracked files included) as a commit at `refs/worktree/callisto/checkpoint/version`, with `HEAD`, a digest of the tags and the Callisto version in its message (`GitAccess::create_checkpoint`, through `CommandRunner::run_with_env` and a temporary index). It is created only if absent (E341), never signed, and deleted after the last effect, including the emitted decision and preview.
-- A run that finds the checkpoint checks it out in a temporary worktree (with `HEAD` on the commit the run started from, so commit inference reads real history), loads the workspace there and plans as usual with the date the interrupted run used. `resume_input_changes` then applies that plan in the worktree, without a lockfile refresh, to get the tree a finished run leaves. Every file the plan was derived from (the manifests the loader opened, `callisto.toml`, the changesets) and every file it writes must be on disk either as the checkpoint recorded it or as that expected tree has it, compared through Git's filters, and a file the expected tree lacks counts as the run's deletion only when the index lacks it too; `HEAD`, the tags, the Callisto version and the workspace must match. Anything else refuses with E340, whose help names the way out: delete the checkpoint ref and follow the E121 steps. Only then is the plan applied to the real tree. A dry run has no permit to apply in the worktree, so it reports the plan without this check. The apply's preflight and workspace resolution run before the checkpoint is created (`apply::validate_plan`), so a run that cannot start leaves no checkpoint, and a plan that writes nothing records none. The checkpoint's temporary index starts from a copy of the real one and adds the working tree tolerating unreadable files; the scratch checkout runs none of the user's hooks.
+- A run that finds the checkpoint checks it out in a temporary worktree (with `HEAD` on the commit the run started from, so commit inference reads real history), loads the workspace there and plans as usual with the date the interrupted run used. `resume_input_changes` then applies that plan in the worktree, without a lockfile refresh, to get the tree a finished run leaves. Every file the plan was derived from (the manifests the loader opened, `callisto.toml`, the changesets) and every file it writes must be on disk either as the checkpoint recorded it or as that expected tree has it, compared through Git's filters, and a file the expected tree lacks counts as the run's deletion only when the index lacks it too; `HEAD`, the tags, the Callisto version and the workspace must match, and the temporary worktree directory the plan came from must hold this workspace's tree in the checkpoint, so a plan made in another directory is never applied. Anything else refuses with E340, whose help names the way out: delete the checkpoint ref and follow the E121 steps. Only then is the plan applied to the real tree. A dry run has no permit to apply in the worktree, so it reports the plan without this check. The apply's preflight and workspace resolution run before the checkpoint is created (`apply::validate_plan`), so a run that cannot start leaves no checkpoint, and a plan that writes nothing records none. The checkpoint's temporary index starts from a copy of the real one and adds the working tree tolerating unreadable files; the scratch checkout runs none of the user's hooks.
 - E121 still guards states no checkpoint recorded.

 - Each `PlannedBump` carries `from` and `to`. Before writing a manifest (or a platform manifest), apply reads its current version: `from` means write, `to` means already done, anything else is `UnexpectedManifestVersion`.
